# Tooling and Dependencies

## Dependencies

Before adding a package, inspect the existing stack for the capability. Explain
and resolve an architectural choice before introducing an alternative to an
established framework, package manager, or tool.

Change dependency declarations through the project's existing package manager.
Regenerate lockfiles with that tool rather than editing resolved versions by
hand. Keep related manifest and lockfile changes together. Preserve private
package-source configuration and keep credentials out of committed files.

## Validation configuration

Use the existing compiler, formatter, linter, and test configuration. Fix code
instead of disabling checks to hide a specific failure. Any necessary exception
should be narrow, justified, and consistent with project policy.

Inspect current hooks and CI before changing them. Preserve required checks;
do not bypass hooks as a workaround. Place checks according to the project's
existing workflow instead of assuming every project needs the same hook setup.

## Scripts and environments

Match the established scripting language, file placement, and runtime setup.
Resolve required paths reliably instead of depending on an undocumented working
directory. Read secrets and deployment values through the project's existing
configuration mechanism; report missing required inputs clearly.
